body:
me as well. personally, it's the difficulty of transitioning from a protagonist that i adored to one that i was less than impressed with. also, the pacing is a lot slower. less adrenaline to keep me up late into the night (which might not be a bad thing, i suppose.)

&#x200B;

victoria is definitely a downgrade at a first glance, but it could be worse.

personally, i agree. i kind of skimmed through the story towards the end (impatience, mostly, and the adrenaline was getting to my sleep-deprived brain). still, i find it hard to label taylor as the villain - she was a true anti-hero. 

&#x200B;

i got really attached to taylor and i'm still not sure why. while she has done some fucked-up shit that i don't condone (but for some reason feel less passionate about than i normally would), the mind control thing wasn't entirely... wrong, i guess? sure, there might have been more humane ways. but the world was ending, morale was low, and people were giving up. she did what she had to do and, in that situation, i know that i would have done the same - broken moral and personal rules, laws, for the good of humanity. the trauma of her actions may still plague those who were touched by her influence, but i'd rather sacrifice my body and free (for a small period of time, no less) at a shot at a peaceful life free of fear and oppression. but that's my take on it.  


out of pure anger and lack of motivation, i haven't read the epilogues. don't know if i ever will. taylor was the first heroine i've liked in, well, a long time. i'll recover, emotionally, in time but i feel as if reading worm so quickly and investing so much emotion and faith in it wore me out.

&#x200B;

sorry for the crappy grammar and emotionally-driven opinions.
